    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in a log cabin in Kentucky and largely self-educated, Lincoln became a lawyer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> legislator and returned to politics in 1854 to oppose the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> into the territories.</li><li>Elected in 1860 as the first Republican president, he took executive control of the Union's war against the seceded Confederate states, and on January 1, 1863, declared the slaves in the states in rebellion to be free.</li><li>He was shot five days after the Confederate surrender at Appomattox; later ages have honoured him as a martyr and the Great Emancipator, and some have disputed that title.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1849 Lincoln received a patent for a flotation device for moving riverboats in shallow water. It was never commercialized, but it made him the only president to hold a patent.</p></aside>
    </section>

