    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1809</span><span class="rf-text">Lincoln is born in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1834</span><span class="rf-text">In his second campaign he wins a seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> House of Representatives, where he serves four terms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1846</span><span class="rf-text">He is elected to the U.S. House of Representatives as the only Whig in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> delegation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1858</span><span class="rf-text">He holds seven debates with Stephen A. Douglas in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> Senate campaign; the legislature re-elects Douglas</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1860</span><span class="rf-text">He is elected the first Republican president with 39.8 percent of the vote in a four-way race, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> adopts an ordinance of secession</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1863</span><span class="rf-text">He issues the final <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a> on January 1 and delivers the Gettysburg Address on November 19</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1864</span><span class="rf-text">He is re-elected with 55.1 percent of the popular vote and 212 electoral votes to George B. McClellan's 21</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1865</span><span class="rf-text">John Wilkes Booth shoots him at Ford's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> on April 14, and he dies the next morning</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abraham Lincoln was born on February 12, 1809, in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky, and was raised on Sinking Spring Farm and Knob Creek Farm near Hodgenville. He was the second child of Thomas Lincoln and Nancy Hanks Lincoln. His father, a farmer, cabinetmaker and carpenter, bought several farms in Kentucky but could get clear title to none and lost hundreds of acres in legal disputes. In 1816 the family moved to Indiana, where land titles were more reliable. His parents belonged to a Separate Baptist church whose members largely opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p>On October 5, 1818, Nancy died of milk sickness. Thomas married Sarah Bush Johnston, a widow with three children, on December 2, 1819, and Abraham became close to his stepmother. Lincoln was largely self-educated: his schooling came from itinerant teachers and amounted to less than 12 months by the age of 15, but he was an avid reader. His father hired him out to neighbouring farmers and kept the money, as was the custom. A flatboat journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> showed him slave markets that, according to the historian Michael Burlingame, "would leave an indelible impression on him".</p><p>In March 1830 the extended family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. Lincoln grew distant from his father, in part over Thomas's lack of interest in education, and later refused to attend his deathbed or funeral in 1851. From 1831 he made his home in New Salem, where he worked in a general store, served as a militia captain in the Black Hawk War without seeing combat, kept a store that went into debt, and was postmaster and county surveyor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war.jpg" alt="Mexican–American War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nebel_Mexican_War_12_Scott_in_Mexico_City_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adolphe Jean-Baptiste Bayot / Carl Nebel, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lincoln lost his first campaign for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> House of Representatives in 1832 but won in 1834, as a supporter of the Whig leader Henry Clay, and served four terms. He read law on his own, was admitted to the Illinois bar on September 9, 1836, and moved to Springfield. In 1842 he married Mary Todd. Elected to the U.S. House of Representatives in 1846, he spoke against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> and demanded in his "spot resolutions" that President James K. Polk name the spot where the war began, a stand that cost him support in his district. He had pledged to serve only one term and returned to his practice.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>–Nebraska Act of 1854, by which Senator Stephen A. Douglas let the voters of each territory decide the status of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, brought him back to politics. His Peoria Speech of October 1854 was one of an estimated 175 speeches he gave in the next six years on excluding slavery from the territories. He then lost a Senate election in the state legislature, joined the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-party-united-states">Republican Party</a> in 1856, and in 1858 accepted its Senate nomination with the House Divided Speech. His seven debates with Douglas drew thousands; the legislature re-elected Douglas, but the campaign gave Lincoln a national political presence.</p><p>His speech at Cooper Union on February 27, 1860, brought him into contention for the presidency. He won the Republican nomination on the third ballot in Chicago on May 18 and was elected on November 6, 1860, with 1,866,452 votes, or 39.8 percent, in a four-way race. No ballots were cast for him in 10 of the 15 Southern slave states.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant.jpg" alt="Ulysses S. Grant" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulysses_S._Grant_1870-1880_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brady-Handy Photograph Collection, Library of Congress, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before Lincoln took office in March 1861, seven states had adopted ordinances of secession, and six of them declared themselves the Confederate States of America. On April 12, 1861, Confederate forces fired on Fort Sumter. Lincoln called for 75,000 militiamen, and four more states seceded. He took executive control of the war: he imposed a naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> on Confederate ports, disbursed funds before appropriation by Congress and suspended habeas corpus. Dissatisfied with a succession of generals, he replaced George B. McClellan and then Ambrose Burnside, and after victories at Vicksburg and Chattanooga promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> to command all Union armies.</p><p>On September 22, 1862, after the Union victory at Antietam, he issued the preliminary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a>, and on January 1, 1863, the final one, freeing the slaves in the ten states not then under Union control. Enlisting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> became official policy. At Gettysburg on November 19, 1863, he said that the nation was "conceived in liberty and dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal". He pressed Congress for a constitutional amendment outlawing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, which the House passed on January 31, 1865.</p><p>Under him Congress passed the first federal income tax in 1861, authorised paper "greenbacks" in 1862 and created national banks in 1863. The Homestead Act and the Morrill Land-Grant Colleges Act of 1862 and the Pacific Railway Acts supported settlement, agricultural colleges and the first transcontinental railroad. No foreign nation recognised the Confederacy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation.jpg" alt="Emancipation Proclamation" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emancipation_Proclamation.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W. Roberts, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lincoln governed between factions. Copperheads, the anti-war Democrats, attacked his refusal to compromise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a>; Radical Republicans criticised him for moving too slowly to abolish slavery, and he pocket-vetoed their Wade–Davis Bill of 1864. In 1861 he supported the Corwin Amendment, which would have protected slavery in the states where it existed, and in August 1862 he wrote to Horace Greeley that his "paramount object" was "to save the Union, and is not either to save or to destroy slavery".</p><p>His use of war powers was contested. Chief Justice Roger Taney held in Ex parte Merryman that only Congress could suspend habeas corpus; Lincoln persisted. Under various suspensions 15,000 civilians were detained without trial, and several, including the anti-war Democrat Clement L. Vallandigham, were tried in military courts, which was condemned as an attack on free speech. After the Dakota War of 1862, in which hundreds of settlers were killed, a trial that the legal scholar Carol Chomsky called "a study in military injustice" sentenced 303 Dakota to death; Lincoln pardoned all but 39, and 38 were hanged in the largest mass execution in U.S. history.</p><p>Later critics have questioned the title of Great Emancipator. In 1968 Lerone Bennett Jr. called Lincoln a White supremacist, noting that he used ethnic slurs, opposed social equality and proposed that freed slaves voluntarily move to another country. Defenders cast his delays and racist rhetoric as concessions to political necessity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee.jpg" alt="Robert E. Lee" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Edward_Lee_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Julian Vannerson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lincoln ran for re-election in 1864 under the label of the National Union Party, with the War Democrat Andrew Johnson as his running mate, against the former general McClellan. Grant's bloody stalemates damaged his prospects, and he wrote a confidential memorandum pledging to co-operate with the president-elect if he lost. Victories at Atlanta in September and in the Shenandoah Valley in October turned public opinion, and he won 55.1 percent of the popular vote and 212 electoral votes to McClellan's 21. On February 3, 1865, he met Confederate officials at Hampton Roads but refused to negotiate with the Confederacy as a coequal.</p><p>His second inaugural address, on March 4, 1865, closed with the words "With malice toward none; with charity for all". On April 9 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a> surrendered to Grant at Appomattox. In his last public address, on April 11, Lincoln said that the vote should be given to some Black men, "the very intelligent" and "those who serve our cause as soldiers". John Wilkes Booth, an actor and Confederate spy who heard the speech, plotted to kill him.</p><p>On the evening of April 14 Lincoln and his wife attended the play Our American Cousin at Ford's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a>. At 10:15 pm Booth entered the box and shot him in the back of the head. Carried across the street to Petersen House, Lincoln lay in a coma for nine hours and died at 7:22 am on April 15. Andrew Johnson was sworn in as president later that day. After lying in state, Lincoln's body travelled for two weeks by funeral train to Springfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, where it was buried at Oak Ridge Cemetery.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass.jpg" alt="Frederick Douglass" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederick_Douglass_(circa_1879)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Kendall Warren, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The amendment Lincoln had promoted was ratified in December 1865 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a>, abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and involuntary servitude except as a punishment for crime. His assassination made him a national martyr, and abolitionists viewed him as a champion of human liberty. The sociologist Barry Schwartz states that his reputation grew slowly from the late 19th century until the Progressive Era, when he emerged as one of America's most venerated heroes, even among White Southerners; the high point came in 1922 with the dedication of the Lincoln Memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a></p><p>Later ages claimed him in different ways. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a> era liberals honoured him as an advocate of the common man; by the 1970s he had become a hero to political conservatives for his nationalism and his support for business. Schwartz argued in 2008 that his symbolic power has lost relevance since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a> said that in his company "I was never reminded of my humble origin, or of my unpopular color". On Reconstruction, Eric Foner argues that Lincoln did not see it as an opportunity for a sweeping social revolution beyond emancipation, while Phillip S. Paludan suggests he was moving towards a more radical position on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>'s rights.</p><p>In surveys of U.S. scholars since 1948 the top three presidents are generally Lincoln, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>. He has been admired abroad by figures including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, who likened the "new birth of freedom" of the Gettysburg Address to the end of apartheid.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/dixie">Dixie<span>1859</span></a><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body<span>1861</span></a><a href="/songs/battle-cry-of-freedom">Battle Cry of Freedom<span>1862</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Abraham Lincoln rose from a frontier childhood to the presidency in 1860 as an opponent of the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. He led the Union through the Civil War, issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a> and promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a>, while critics attacked his war powers and later his views on race. He was assassinated in April 1865.</em></p>

